// Day 26 — error rate over a five-minute window. This is the ALERT query.
//
// THE MINIMUM-VOLUME FLOOR IS THE WHOLE DESIGN, and without it this alert
// gets muted within a week.
//
// quotes-api-dev runs at minReplicas 0 and sees almost no traffic. In a
// five-minute window with two requests, one failure is a 50% error rate.
// Every deploy, every cold start, every probe of a scaled-to-zero app would
// page someone. An alert that cries wolf is worse than no alert: it trains
// the person receiving it to ignore the one that matters, and it does so
// quietly.
//
// So the query returns NO ROWS below MinRequests, and a scheduled query rule
// with no rows does not fire. The threshold is deliberately expressed as data
// rather than hidden in the rule's configuration, so anyone reading the query
// can see the condition without opening the alert in the portal.
//
// Health probes are excluded for the same reason as in 01: they are the bulk
// of the traffic and they always succeed, so including them would dilute a
// real error rate to below any threshold worth setting. This makes the alert
// STRICTER, not weaker — the floor is measured against real traffic only.
//
// The rule thresholds on the errorRatePct column (metricMeasureColumn), so
// the query returning a row is not by itself the alert condition.
let LookbackMinutes = 5;
let MinRequests     = 20;    // below this, the ratio is noise
AppRequests
| where TimeGenerated > ago(LookbackMinutes * 1m)
| where Name !startswith "GET /health"
| summarize
    total  = sum(ItemCount),
    failed = sumif(ItemCount, Success == false)
| where total >= MinRequests
| extend errorRatePct = round(100.0 * failed / total, 2)
| project errorRatePct, total, failed
